<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  OnScreen talks to a SELF-HOSTED media server, which the operator
  typically runs on a LAN address — `http://192.168.1.50:7070`,
  `http://media.lan:7070`, `http://onscreen-server.local:7070`. Plain
  HTTP on the LAN is the expected deployment shape; Android refuses
  to dial cleartext URLs unless explicitly allowed here. Internet-side
  hops (TMDB, OpenSubtitles, etc.) still go over HTTPS — those URLs
  come from the server, which itself uses a `safehttp`-policy-
  restricted client.

  We accept the `InsecureBaseConfiguration` lint warning consciously:
  flipping cleartext off would break the dominant deployment model
  for a marginal threat (an attacker already on the user's LAN).
  Server-side TLS support is in Settings ▸ Security; operators who
  serve over HTTPS get certificate validation on the client
  automatically.

  Matches the TV-client posture (clients/android/.../network_security_config.xml)
  — including the system-only trust store. Keep the two files in step; a
  divergence here is invisible at runtime and voids TLS on whichever side lags.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!--
              SYSTEM CAs ONLY. `<certificates src="user" />` used to be here; it is
              deliberately gone, mirroring the TV client.

              Android stopped trusting the user CA store by default in API 24
              precisely because it is the easiest MITM foothold on the platform: a
              sideloaded "helper" app that walks the owner through installing a
              profile, an MDM / work-profile enrolment, or a few minutes of ADB
              access is enough to plant one. With that anchor in place every HTTPS
              call to the server was interceptable, which means the login password
              and the PASETO access / stream / asset tokens were all readable by
              whoever planted the CA.

              This mattered more on the phone than anywhere else. normalizeServerUrl
              (ui/pair/PairViewModel.kt) deliberately routes every non-RFC1918 host
              to https for Cloudflare-tunnel / Tailscale-Funnel deployments, so for
              those users TLS is the ONLY confidentiality control — and the first
              thing on the wire is POST /auth/login carrying the plaintext account
              password, which unlike a token cannot be revoked.

              The anchor's old rationale (an admin with a self-signed cert gets
              chain validation) did not hold: it sat in base-config, so the planted
              CA was trusted for EVERY host the app dials, and no self-signed-cert
              flow exists in the app to depend on it. If one is ever added, it
              belongs in a <domain-config> scoped to the operator's own host, not
              here.

              The cleartext allowance above is a considered trade for the LAN
              deployment model. This was not.
            -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
